DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The  function  ccaattooppeenn()  opens a message catalog and returns a catalog
       descriptor.  The descriptor  remains  valid  until  ccaattcclloossee()  or  eexx‐‐
       eeccvvee(2).   If  a  file descriptor is used to implement catalog descrip‐
       tors, then the FFDD__CCLLOOEEXXEECC flag will be set.

       The argument _n_a_m_e specifies the name  of  the  message  catalog  to  be
       opened.   If  _n_a_m_e  specifies  an absolute path (i.e., contains a '/'),
       then _n_a_m_e specifies a pathname for the message catalog.  Otherwise, the
       environment variable NNLLSSPPAATTHH is used with _n_a_m_e substituted for %%NN  (see
       llooccaallee(7)).   It  is  unspecified whether NNLLSSPPAATTHH will be used when the
       process has root privileges.  If NNLLSSPPAATTHH does not exist in the environ‐
       ment, or if a message catalog cannot be opened  in  any  of  the  paths
       specified  by  it,  then  an implementation defined path is used.  This
       latter default path may depend on the LLCC__MMEESSSSAAGGEESS locale  setting  when
       the _f_l_a_g argument is NNLL__CCAATT__LLOOCCAALLEE and on the LLAANNGG environment variable
       when  the _f_l_a_g argument is 0.  Changing the LLCC__MMEESSSSAAGGEESS part of the lo‐
       cale may invalidate open catalog descriptors.

       The _f_l_a_g argument to ccaattooppeenn() is used to indicate the source  for  the
       language  to  use.  If it is set to NNLL__CCAATT__LLOOCCAALLEE, then it will use the
       current locale setting for LLCC__MMEESSSSAAGGEESS.  Otherwise,  it  will  use  the
       LLAANNGG environment variable.

       The  function ccaattcclloossee() closes the message catalog identified by _c_a_t_a_‐
       _l_o_g.  It invalidates any subsequent references to the  message  catalog
